WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.880
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:04.843 --> 00:00:05.760
KYLE STORY: All right.

00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:07.760
Today we're going to be
telling you a little bit

00:00:07.760 --> 00:00:10.920
about understanding the earth,
machine learning, and then

00:00:10.920 --> 00:00:14.400
a relatively new rule tool
called Kubeflow Pipelines.

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:16.680
I'm Kyle Story, a
computer vision engineer

00:00:16.680 --> 00:00:18.080
and Descartes Labs.

00:00:18.080 --> 00:00:19.163
And my co-presenter--

00:00:19.163 --> 00:00:19.830
FAUSTINE LI: Hi.

00:00:19.830 --> 00:00:20.760
I'm Faustine Li.

00:00:20.760 --> 00:00:23.245
I'm also a computer
vision engineer.

00:00:23.245 --> 00:00:25.120
KYLE STORY: And with
that, let's get started.

00:00:25.120 --> 00:00:28.560
So we're going to run through a
lot of things during this talk.

00:00:28.560 --> 00:00:32.250
The three key points that we
hope that you can walk away

00:00:32.250 --> 00:00:35.550
from this room remembering
are that in the domain

00:00:35.550 --> 00:00:37.610
that we work in,
satellite imagery,

00:00:37.610 --> 00:00:40.950
there is an incredibly large
amount and a growing amount

00:00:40.950 --> 00:00:42.210
of satellite imagery.

00:00:42.210 --> 00:00:43.950
And it provides a
very powerful way

00:00:43.950 --> 00:00:46.530
of understanding
the world around us.

00:00:46.530 --> 00:00:50.880
Second, machine learning is
a very powerful technique

00:00:50.880 --> 00:00:54.990
for processing and understanding
the satellite imagery.

00:00:54.990 --> 00:00:58.080
But it presents unique
challenges in this domain.

00:00:58.080 --> 00:01:02.310
And third, we are
using an array of tools

00:01:02.310 --> 00:01:05.340
within the Google
Cloud suite to be

00:01:05.340 --> 00:01:07.530
able to understand
these imagery,

00:01:07.530 --> 00:01:08.940
provide value for our customers.

00:01:08.940 --> 00:01:11.610
And in particular, we've been
exploring Kubeflow Pipelines,

00:01:11.610 --> 00:01:15.820
so we'd like to tell you a
little bit about that today.

00:01:15.820 --> 00:01:18.817
So what do you think of
when you think of the Earth?

00:01:18.817 --> 00:01:19.650
What do you picture?

00:01:19.650 --> 00:01:24.390
Perhaps it's this iconic
picture from the Apollo space

00:01:24.390 --> 00:01:27.600
mission of almost 50
years ago, at this point,

00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:29.480
a blue dot out in space.

00:01:29.480 --> 00:01:34.140
Well, since that time technology
has allowed satellites

00:01:34.140 --> 00:01:37.410
to take an increasingly
large amount of data covering

00:01:37.410 --> 00:01:40.470
our globe through a wide
variety of spectrums,

00:01:40.470 --> 00:01:44.740
and over a regular
time interval.

00:01:44.740 --> 00:01:46.230
And what Descartes Labs--

00:01:46.230 --> 00:01:49.750
we're in the business of taking
that massive amount of data,

00:01:49.750 --> 00:01:52.660
using the Google
Cloud as a backbone,

00:01:52.660 --> 00:01:55.540
and processing that into
valuable information

00:01:55.540 --> 00:01:57.670
and decisions that our
customers can make.

00:01:57.670 --> 00:02:00.550
So just to give
you a sense of what

00:02:00.550 --> 00:02:01.960
we can do with
satellite imagery,

00:02:01.960 --> 00:02:04.900
for example, in this
picture on the left hand

00:02:04.900 --> 00:02:07.660
we've used a particular
combination of spectral bands

00:02:07.660 --> 00:02:10.690
that pull out vegetation, allow
us to understand crops, crop

00:02:10.690 --> 00:02:11.830
development.

00:02:11.830 --> 00:02:15.670
In the center, this
synthetic aperture radar band

00:02:15.670 --> 00:02:18.400
allows us to understand
built infrastructure

00:02:18.400 --> 00:02:22.990
for mines and minerals further
to the right, and finally high

00:02:22.990 --> 00:02:25.060
resolution satellite
imagery, allowing

00:02:25.060 --> 00:02:27.730
us to study and understand,
in this case, oil

00:02:27.730 --> 00:02:28.820
and gas infrastructure.

00:02:28.820 --> 00:02:32.320
These are fracking
installations.

00:02:32.320 --> 00:02:35.800
Outside of active sensors
in the visible bands,

00:02:35.800 --> 00:02:36.640
they're are all--

00:02:36.640 --> 00:02:38.050
sorry, passive sensors.

00:02:38.050 --> 00:02:39.460
There are also active sensors.

00:02:39.460 --> 00:02:41.630
For example, here is
synthetic aperture radar.

00:02:41.630 --> 00:02:43.480
So this is actually
a satellite that's

00:02:43.480 --> 00:02:46.120
flying over, sending
down a radar signal,

00:02:46.120 --> 00:02:48.500
receiving the reflected signal.

00:02:48.500 --> 00:02:51.600
And so that allows us to
both see the reflections off

00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:54.670
of metal structures or you
can see all the boats flowing

00:02:54.670 --> 00:02:58.290
into this harbor, and also
learn about 3D information

00:02:58.290 --> 00:03:00.820
in this picture.

00:03:00.820 --> 00:03:04.120
That all is to say that there
is an incredible amount of data

00:03:04.120 --> 00:03:06.850
that's being produced all
the time from satellites

00:03:06.850 --> 00:03:09.100
and other sources.

00:03:09.100 --> 00:03:11.470
That data gives us a
really valuable picture

00:03:11.470 --> 00:03:13.510
for understanding
the world around us

00:03:13.510 --> 00:03:15.070
and deriving actual insights.

00:03:15.070 --> 00:03:18.100
This is a picture that was
taken by the Sentinel satellite

00:03:18.100 --> 00:03:20.890
shortly after the Camp fire
erupted in northern California

00:03:20.890 --> 00:03:22.210
in November.

00:03:22.210 --> 00:03:26.080
You can see the plumes of
smoke coming off of the place

00:03:26.080 --> 00:03:27.340
where the fire is burning.

00:03:27.340 --> 00:03:32.290
And by looking into different
spectral wavelengths

00:03:32.290 --> 00:03:34.240
than our eye can
see, we can actually

00:03:34.240 --> 00:03:36.920
pick out where the fire
is actively burning.

00:03:36.920 --> 00:03:40.090
This is a shortwave infrared
and a near infrared false color

00:03:40.090 --> 00:03:40.910
image.

00:03:40.910 --> 00:03:43.720
We can see where the fires act
is actually actively burning,

00:03:43.720 --> 00:03:45.190
in this case.

00:03:45.190 --> 00:03:47.110
And so by using
satellite imagery,

00:03:47.110 --> 00:03:48.820
that allows us to
really understand

00:03:48.820 --> 00:03:52.917
what's going on in our world
in a very powerful way.

00:03:55.720 --> 00:03:59.380
What's important then is turning
that massive amount of data

00:03:59.380 --> 00:04:03.680
into decisions or understanding
that our customers can use.

00:04:03.680 --> 00:04:05.710
So here is an image
of crop fields

00:04:05.710 --> 00:04:11.590
over in the center of the
United States, in Nebraska.

00:04:11.590 --> 00:04:13.930
And we've built
out a pipeline that

00:04:13.930 --> 00:04:17.380
allows us to predict the
amount of corn and soy

00:04:17.380 --> 00:04:19.420
that are produced in a
given growing season.

00:04:19.420 --> 00:04:22.210
And then that helps our
customers, in particular cargo,

00:04:22.210 --> 00:04:24.863
be able to understand how
they move grain around,

00:04:24.863 --> 00:04:26.530
and then what they
can expect for prices

00:04:26.530 --> 00:04:29.630
in the agricultural market.

00:04:29.630 --> 00:04:31.550
As I mentioned
earlier on, we find

00:04:31.550 --> 00:04:35.150
that computer vision and machine
learning is a very powerful way

00:04:35.150 --> 00:04:38.720
of understanding these data
sets and being able to produce

00:04:38.720 --> 00:04:41.250
insights at scale.

00:04:41.250 --> 00:04:44.360
And that's what the
remainder of this talk

00:04:44.360 --> 00:04:46.670
will largely focus on.

00:04:46.670 --> 00:04:49.760
So within satellite imagery
and computer vision,

00:04:49.760 --> 00:04:53.390
we think about these
along three axes--

00:04:53.390 --> 00:04:55.320
detect, map, and monitor.

00:04:55.320 --> 00:04:58.400
So detect is using
computer vision machine

00:04:58.400 --> 00:05:00.590
learning to be able to
find infrastructure, find

00:05:00.590 --> 00:05:02.507
the points of interest,
the things that you're

00:05:02.507 --> 00:05:05.630
interested in all
around the globe,

00:05:05.630 --> 00:05:08.030
mapping those across,
so mapping those

00:05:08.030 --> 00:05:11.180
across the globe in the
different regions of interest

00:05:11.180 --> 00:05:13.290
that you're interested in.

00:05:13.290 --> 00:05:16.280
And then finally, being able
to monitor those in real time,

00:05:16.280 --> 00:05:18.260
being able to
understand changes,

00:05:18.260 --> 00:05:21.720
and then use that to
drive your business.

00:05:21.720 --> 00:05:23.390
So for an example
of detection we've

00:05:23.390 --> 00:05:25.380
built a computer
vision algorithm

00:05:25.380 --> 00:05:27.870
that can detect wind turbines.

00:05:27.870 --> 00:05:30.030
And we can now run this
anywhere in the world.

00:05:30.030 --> 00:05:31.890
We're able to use
Google Cloud to map

00:05:31.890 --> 00:05:36.290
all of the wind turbines in
the United States over night.

00:05:36.290 --> 00:05:39.770
Mapping-- so this is a
convolutional neural net that

00:05:39.770 --> 00:05:41.720
maps out the
footprints of buildings

00:05:41.720 --> 00:05:44.125
in high resolution
satellite imagery.

00:05:44.125 --> 00:05:45.500
You can see the
satellite imagery

00:05:45.500 --> 00:05:50.000
on the left, and then the output
of this neural net algorithm

00:05:50.000 --> 00:05:53.160
in the middle and on the right.

00:05:53.160 --> 00:05:55.410
And then finally,
monitoring change over time.

00:05:55.410 --> 00:05:58.160
So this is a pixel-based
random forest algorithm

00:05:58.160 --> 00:06:01.250
that uses the spectral
information from the Sentinel

00:06:01.250 --> 00:06:03.680
satellite to be able
to make a mask of where

00:06:03.680 --> 00:06:05.330
water is at any time.

00:06:05.330 --> 00:06:07.040
And what you're
seeing here is running

00:06:07.040 --> 00:06:11.108
that over sequential time
slices of satellite imagery.

00:06:11.108 --> 00:06:13.400
And you're seeing this
reservoir in northern California

00:06:13.400 --> 00:06:18.272
dry up during the drought
of the previous few years.

00:06:18.272 --> 00:06:19.730
So with that, I'd
like to hand over

00:06:19.730 --> 00:06:23.060
the rest of the presentations
to my co-presenter, Faustine,

00:06:23.060 --> 00:06:25.340
to dig into all the things
that I've been presenting

00:06:25.340 --> 00:06:27.530
in a little more detail.

00:06:27.530 --> 00:06:29.030
FAUSTINE LI: Thanks, Kyle.

00:06:29.030 --> 00:06:31.850
So I want to preface
my part of the talk

00:06:31.850 --> 00:06:35.210
by saying we're not
really all that interested

00:06:35.210 --> 00:06:39.500
in digging into the
algorithms behind our models.

00:06:39.500 --> 00:06:42.260
If you're interested in
domain-specific algorithms,

00:06:42.260 --> 00:06:45.130
Kyle gave a great talk
at last year's NeXT.

00:06:45.130 --> 00:06:48.080
But for this talk, what
we found as scientists

00:06:48.080 --> 00:06:52.100
was that we really couldn't
treat the model as a black box,

00:06:52.100 --> 00:06:54.890
even though we really wanted to.

00:06:54.890 --> 00:06:57.470
That data goes in
and answers come out.

00:06:57.470 --> 00:06:59.660
Well, what happens to
the data and how does it

00:06:59.660 --> 00:07:01.040
arrive in our model?

00:07:01.040 --> 00:07:03.290
And what do we do
with the outputs?

00:07:03.290 --> 00:07:05.700
Where do we store
it, and so forth?

00:07:05.700 --> 00:07:09.500
So what we found was that we
should focus on our workflows

00:07:09.500 --> 00:07:10.760
and how to streamline those.

00:07:10.760 --> 00:07:13.990
So let me step through this.

00:07:13.990 --> 00:07:17.070
So there's a lot of things that
happen after the imagery comes

00:07:17.070 --> 00:07:18.180
off of the satellite.

00:07:18.180 --> 00:07:22.440
We have a great ingest team that
ingests data into our platform

00:07:22.440 --> 00:07:24.750
that we built on
top of Google Cloud.

00:07:24.750 --> 00:07:27.360
But, of course, imagery
is completely useless

00:07:27.360 --> 00:07:28.750
if we can't access it.

00:07:28.750 --> 00:07:32.940
So we host microservices
that allow scientists

00:07:32.940 --> 00:07:37.770
to programmatically
query and access imagery.

00:07:37.770 --> 00:07:40.710
And we interact with
those microservices

00:07:40.710 --> 00:07:44.590
using our Descartes
Labs Python Library.

00:07:44.590 --> 00:07:47.620
Once we have training data,
it's time to train our model.

00:07:47.620 --> 00:07:52.030
And we build convolutional
neural networks in TensorFlow.

00:07:52.030 --> 00:07:55.090
And we heavily leverage
Google Compute Engine

00:07:55.090 --> 00:07:56.890
to train our models.

00:07:56.890 --> 00:07:59.770
And we found that deep
learning VMs are really

00:07:59.770 --> 00:08:00.990
fast to get us started.

00:08:03.690 --> 00:08:06.110
But, of course, now that
we're ready with our model

00:08:06.110 --> 00:08:07.220
it's time to deploy.

00:08:07.220 --> 00:08:09.620
And for large
geospatial applications,

00:08:09.620 --> 00:08:13.980
that often means inference over
multiple regions of interest.

00:08:13.980 --> 00:08:19.880
And we actually have a task
system that we built and host.

00:08:19.880 --> 00:08:23.150
And that allows us to
scale out our models

00:08:23.150 --> 00:08:25.790
by 100 or 1,000 times.

00:08:25.790 --> 00:08:30.690
And I'll talk a little bit
more about this in depth later.

00:08:30.690 --> 00:08:32.693
So to dive in a
little bit deeper,

00:08:32.693 --> 00:08:34.110
I don't want to
belabor the point.

00:08:34.110 --> 00:08:37.039
But our imagery is
not just pixels.

00:08:37.039 --> 00:08:40.700
In fact, it has a lot of
Earth science related to it.

00:08:40.700 --> 00:08:43.730
And so prior to
analysis, we have

00:08:43.730 --> 00:08:47.690
to georegister and co-locate
all of our imagery.

00:08:47.690 --> 00:08:50.240
And we also have to
care about things

00:08:50.240 --> 00:08:53.650
like atmospheric reflectants
or whether clouds are obscuring

00:08:53.650 --> 00:08:54.650
our objects of interest.

00:08:57.660 --> 00:09:02.840
Once our imagery is analytics
ready, we have to access it.

00:09:02.840 --> 00:09:06.200
And because our
data platform holds

00:09:06.200 --> 00:09:09.920
large, multi-modal data
sets, we have APIs that

00:09:09.920 --> 00:09:12.630
make it really easy to access.

00:09:12.630 --> 00:09:17.360
So in four lines of Python code
we get imagery over New Mexico,

00:09:17.360 --> 00:09:19.160
almost instantaneously.

00:09:19.160 --> 00:09:21.590
And this is really
great for developers.

00:09:21.590 --> 00:09:24.440
Because it means that
if I want imagery

00:09:24.440 --> 00:09:26.950
over a different satellite, I
just change one line of code.

00:09:29.990 --> 00:09:32.720
But deployment is
where it gets tricky.

00:09:32.720 --> 00:09:35.690
Often we might want
every square kilometer

00:09:35.690 --> 00:09:38.030
of a very large
region of interest,

00:09:38.030 --> 00:09:40.490
like the United States.

00:09:40.490 --> 00:09:42.390
So how do we do this?

00:09:42.390 --> 00:09:44.390
We have a task
system that we built

00:09:44.390 --> 00:09:49.490
that allows us to scale these
highly elastic workflows.

00:09:49.490 --> 00:09:51.800
And the way we do it
is we have a client

00:09:51.800 --> 00:09:56.000
that creates tiles over the
earth of arbitrary size,

00:09:56.000 --> 00:10:00.300
and each tile has a
hash associated with it.

00:10:00.300 --> 00:10:02.510
We have a deployment
script that knows

00:10:02.510 --> 00:10:06.290
how to instantiate a model, how
to predict, how to pre-process

00:10:06.290 --> 00:10:09.430
and post-process that data.

00:10:09.430 --> 00:10:11.880
And we feed it into
our task's API,

00:10:11.880 --> 00:10:13.380
and that all runs in Kubernetes.

00:10:13.380 --> 00:10:15.790
So the Earth is
massively paralyzable.

00:10:15.790 --> 00:10:20.040
All you have to do is chip it
into non-overlapping tiles,

00:10:20.040 --> 00:10:24.360
and each tile goes into a
separate Docker container.

00:10:24.360 --> 00:10:26.580
And this leverages all
the really nice things

00:10:26.580 --> 00:10:30.660
about Kubernetes, like auto
scaling, CPU and GPU node

00:10:30.660 --> 00:10:33.370
pulls, things like that.

00:10:33.370 --> 00:10:34.830
But we want our results back.

00:10:34.830 --> 00:10:38.790
And so we host a catalog API.

00:10:38.790 --> 00:10:44.370
We're populating those APIs,
that REST endpoint as we go,

00:10:44.370 --> 00:10:45.910
asynchronously.

00:10:45.910 --> 00:10:49.368
So at the end, you get a map of
the Earth with your inference

00:10:49.368 --> 00:10:49.910
on top of it.

00:10:52.920 --> 00:10:56.170
So why are we using Kubeflow?

00:10:56.170 --> 00:10:58.720
Well, conceptually
machine learning pipelines

00:10:58.720 --> 00:11:00.260
are pretty straightforward.

00:11:00.260 --> 00:11:01.220
We get data.

00:11:01.220 --> 00:11:02.170
We train a model.

00:11:02.170 --> 00:11:04.000
And then we deploy.

00:11:04.000 --> 00:11:06.790
But we found that really,
that's not the case.

00:11:06.790 --> 00:11:12.660
And research projects become
quickly unmaintainable.

00:11:12.660 --> 00:11:14.700
And I didn't really talk
about model training,

00:11:14.700 --> 00:11:17.580
because it's probably the
least inspiring part of what

00:11:17.580 --> 00:11:19.190
we do as scientists.

00:11:19.190 --> 00:11:24.390
Currently we sort of manage
our VM infrastructure.

00:11:24.390 --> 00:11:26.760
We have to manage our own GPUs.

00:11:26.760 --> 00:11:29.080
We have to make sure that
Kuta is properly installed.

00:11:29.080 --> 00:11:30.430
and so on.

00:11:30.430 --> 00:11:34.050
And we spend a lot of time
debugging things like this

00:11:34.050 --> 00:11:35.760
instead of focusing
on the science.

00:11:35.760 --> 00:11:39.770
And that's what we really want.

00:11:39.770 --> 00:11:44.130
And this is a really awesome
thing, this task system.

00:11:44.130 --> 00:11:48.410
But the deploy script is
pretty monolithic and hard

00:11:48.410 --> 00:11:49.160
to maintain.

00:11:49.160 --> 00:11:51.980
And we really want
to be able to have

00:11:51.980 --> 00:11:56.190
componentized our deployments.

00:11:56.190 --> 00:12:01.200
So with those pain
points and many more--

00:12:01.200 --> 00:12:05.810
for example, we had
difficulties with managing

00:12:05.810 --> 00:12:08.860
the training environment versus
the deployment environment.

00:12:08.860 --> 00:12:13.650
And we had ran into hard
to debug bugs with that.

00:12:13.650 --> 00:12:17.490
Kubeflow Pipelines seemed
like a great option for us.

00:12:17.490 --> 00:12:20.040
It was immediately attractive,
because the main points

00:12:20.040 --> 00:12:22.530
of Kubeflow are things
that we are running into

00:12:22.530 --> 00:12:24.600
and we want to solve internally.

00:12:24.600 --> 00:12:26.370
So what is Kubeflow Pipelines?

00:12:26.370 --> 00:12:29.512
It is a machine
learning workflow that--

00:12:29.512 --> 00:12:33.330
a machine learning system
that manages machine learning

00:12:33.330 --> 00:12:36.520
workflows on Kubernetes.

00:12:36.520 --> 00:12:39.040
And it aims to make
machine learning

00:12:39.040 --> 00:12:44.860
reusable and reproducible,
and takes a machine

00:12:44.860 --> 00:12:47.080
learning product all the
way from experimentation

00:12:47.080 --> 00:12:48.900
to deployment.

00:12:48.900 --> 00:12:52.660
And, of course, it runs natively
on Kubernetes, which is great.

00:12:52.660 --> 00:12:54.460
Because all of
our infrastructure

00:12:54.460 --> 00:12:57.160
runs on the cloud,
on Kubernetes,

00:12:57.160 --> 00:12:59.020
and it works really
well with our APIs.

00:13:01.570 --> 00:13:04.720
So specifically
about Pipelines--

00:13:04.720 --> 00:13:07.720
the core unit of
work in Pipelines

00:13:07.720 --> 00:13:09.550
is a operation or component.

00:13:09.550 --> 00:13:13.120
And they all run in
a Docker container.

00:13:13.120 --> 00:13:17.320
And each component has
an output and input,

00:13:17.320 --> 00:13:19.950
and we do graph inference.

00:13:19.950 --> 00:13:24.280
So it's a directic
acyclic graph of work,

00:13:24.280 --> 00:13:26.470
and it leverages all
the wonderful things

00:13:26.470 --> 00:13:32.680
about Kubernetes, like
auto scaling, like metrics,

00:13:32.680 --> 00:13:34.410
and so on.

00:13:34.410 --> 00:13:37.590
So this is kind of what
a Pipeline looks like.

00:13:37.590 --> 00:13:41.630
It's basically a Python
function with operations inside.

00:13:41.630 --> 00:13:44.647
And we know that
this is a Pipeline

00:13:44.647 --> 00:13:46.230
because we add a
little decorator that

00:13:46.230 --> 00:13:49.040
says DSL Pipeline.

00:13:49.040 --> 00:13:52.280
And all this Pipeline does
is it downloads a text

00:13:52.280 --> 00:13:55.970
file from a Google
bucket and prints it out.

00:13:55.970 --> 00:13:58.750
And I'll show you this
later in the demo.

00:13:58.750 --> 00:14:01.150
It says Hello, Google Next.

00:14:01.150 --> 00:14:01.650
OK.

00:14:01.650 --> 00:14:03.450
So here's the fun part.

00:14:03.450 --> 00:14:08.880
I wrote a end to end machine
learning Pipeline in Kubeflow.

00:14:08.880 --> 00:14:11.130
And I'm going to
demo it really soon.

00:14:11.130 --> 00:14:14.430
But the application will be
detecting oil and gas sites

00:14:14.430 --> 00:14:16.390
in the Permian Basin.

00:14:16.390 --> 00:14:17.640
These are what they look like.

00:14:17.640 --> 00:14:19.783
They're called well pads.

00:14:19.783 --> 00:14:21.325
If you don't know
what a well pad is,

00:14:21.325 --> 00:14:23.550
it's basically a large
concrete rectangle

00:14:23.550 --> 00:14:26.130
that they put equipment
on while they're

00:14:26.130 --> 00:14:27.330
drilling for oil and gas.

00:14:27.330 --> 00:14:29.340
And we might be
interested in looking

00:14:29.340 --> 00:14:34.170
for these because we might be
interested in monitoring where

00:14:34.170 --> 00:14:38.910
fracking activity
is, or by seeing

00:14:38.910 --> 00:14:43.770
where they're being built
we can monitor our energy

00:14:43.770 --> 00:14:46.140
infrastructure.

00:14:46.140 --> 00:14:49.160
So we want to turn
this into this.

00:14:49.160 --> 00:14:50.550
So how do we do that?

00:14:50.550 --> 00:14:56.420
Well, thankfully, I wrote a end
to end Pipeline in Kubeflow.

00:14:56.420 --> 00:15:00.830
So it goes from pulling the
imagery from our platform

00:15:00.830 --> 00:15:07.400
from just simple labels, to
deploying on our task system

00:15:07.400 --> 00:15:09.690
and seeing the output.

00:15:09.690 --> 00:15:10.190
OK.

00:15:10.190 --> 00:15:13.880
So when the demo
is running, you'll

00:15:13.880 --> 00:15:17.150
be able to see that this
Pipeline does both training

00:15:17.150 --> 00:15:20.260
and deployment.

00:15:20.260 --> 00:15:23.030
And there's some things that
we really like about Pipelines.

00:15:23.030 --> 00:15:27.320
So, for example, each component
friends its own Docker

00:15:27.320 --> 00:15:30.328
container, which means that we
can encapsulate the environment

00:15:30.328 --> 00:15:31.370
that we're working on it.

00:15:31.370 --> 00:15:35.870
And we really can control
the very specific packages

00:15:35.870 --> 00:15:36.920
that we put in it.

00:15:36.920 --> 00:15:40.830
So we can make sure that
our training and deployment

00:15:40.830 --> 00:15:42.710
environments always match.

00:15:42.710 --> 00:15:46.670
And because each component
is containerized,

00:15:46.670 --> 00:15:49.250
we can mix and match components.

00:15:49.250 --> 00:15:50.510
So it promotes reuse.

00:15:53.690 --> 00:15:56.370
And there's some neat
things about Pipelines

00:15:56.370 --> 00:15:58.830
that I hope to show you.

00:15:58.830 --> 00:16:03.123
So they have native
TensorBoard integration,

00:16:03.123 --> 00:16:04.040
which is really great.

00:16:04.040 --> 00:16:08.280
Because you can inspect how
your model's performing,

00:16:08.280 --> 00:16:10.700
and you can compare
different runs.

00:16:10.700 --> 00:16:13.530
So it really supports
the experimentation side

00:16:13.530 --> 00:16:14.280
of model building.

00:16:17.860 --> 00:16:20.480
And there's some great things
about deploying with Pipelines.

00:16:20.480 --> 00:16:24.728
So it leverages all the
Kubernetes infrastructure

00:16:24.728 --> 00:16:25.645
that we have in-house.

00:16:28.260 --> 00:16:32.010
It interopts well with
the tools that we use.

00:16:32.010 --> 00:16:38.460
So we heavily use Stackdriver
and ISTIO, and Spinnaker.

00:16:38.460 --> 00:16:41.610
So all those things
work with Kubeflow.

00:16:41.610 --> 00:16:48.240
And managing identities
and different workflows

00:16:48.240 --> 00:16:49.770
with different
scientists is really

00:16:49.770 --> 00:16:55.320
easy with Kubernetes or
Google Cloud and Kubernetes

00:16:55.320 --> 00:16:56.542
authentication.

00:16:59.380 --> 00:17:00.870
And I didn't really
mention this.

00:17:00.870 --> 00:17:02.430
This is not the
focus of this talk.

00:17:02.430 --> 00:17:05.760
But what we really
found great was

00:17:05.760 --> 00:17:10.260
that Kubeflow Pipelines are a
first class citizen and a AI

00:17:10.260 --> 00:17:11.490
hub.

00:17:11.490 --> 00:17:16.890
And it's a problem internally
where we onboard new scientists

00:17:16.890 --> 00:17:19.079
and we want to get
them up to speed.

00:17:19.079 --> 00:17:20.170
And how do we do that?

00:17:20.170 --> 00:17:23.310
Well, previously we had to
point them to a GitHub repo

00:17:23.310 --> 00:17:26.790
and say install these
packages, run this code.

00:17:26.790 --> 00:17:30.420
And it's really awesome
to be able to point

00:17:30.420 --> 00:17:34.560
to somebody in AI hub and
say download this pipeline.

00:17:34.560 --> 00:17:37.400
Run it in this cluster.

00:17:37.400 --> 00:17:39.540
And it's very, very
straightforward now.

00:17:42.740 --> 00:17:43.240
OK.

00:17:43.240 --> 00:17:49.750
Well, I guess I will close
off this talk, the demo is not

00:17:49.750 --> 00:17:57.100
working, and contextualize what
we're trying to do in the--

00:17:57.100 --> 00:17:58.450
oh, well, the demo is working.

00:17:58.450 --> 00:18:03.010
[LAUGHS] So I will
actually show you this.

00:18:03.010 --> 00:18:07.610
Because this is really awesome.

00:18:07.610 --> 00:18:08.120
OK.

00:18:08.120 --> 00:18:12.410
So this is the UI for
Kubeflow Pipelines.

00:18:12.410 --> 00:18:15.920
And this is that Hello
World Pipeline that I just

00:18:15.920 --> 00:18:18.050
showed you from before.

00:18:18.050 --> 00:18:22.800
And it's as easy
as clicking run.

00:18:22.800 --> 00:18:26.411
So let's call this run Hello.

00:18:26.411 --> 00:18:27.365
We'll click Start.

00:18:31.190 --> 00:18:36.905
It takes a couple seconds for
Kubernetes to schedule some--

00:18:36.905 --> 00:18:38.670
the pods.

00:18:38.670 --> 00:18:41.501
But it should show up
very, very briefly.

00:18:46.220 --> 00:18:46.780
There we go.

00:18:46.780 --> 00:18:47.350
It's running.

00:18:50.270 --> 00:18:52.820
Looks like that completed,
so let's refresh.

00:18:58.180 --> 00:18:59.340
Hello, Google Cloud Next.

00:19:02.080 --> 00:19:04.580
But, you know, that's not
really what we're here for.

00:19:04.580 --> 00:19:07.440
We're here for machine
learning workflows.

00:19:07.440 --> 00:19:09.890
And this is that
oil and well pads

00:19:09.890 --> 00:19:13.290
demo I talked about earlier.

00:19:13.290 --> 00:19:17.810
So it's basically just
as easy as the last one.

00:19:17.810 --> 00:19:18.820
Let's call this Demo.

00:19:21.720 --> 00:19:23.810
I can add a description.

00:19:23.810 --> 00:19:25.130
I can add it to an experiment.

00:19:25.130 --> 00:19:27.140
Let's not do that.

00:19:27.140 --> 00:19:29.860
There's some parameters
that I can set on the fly.

00:19:33.760 --> 00:19:38.780
And then I can click
Start, and it's on its way.

00:19:38.780 --> 00:19:42.770
So the idea behind Kubeflow
is that machine learning

00:19:42.770 --> 00:19:44.400
should be as--

00:19:44.400 --> 00:19:48.950
machine learning should be
as painless as possible.

00:19:48.950 --> 00:19:53.540
And previously, there
wasn't really great tools.

00:19:53.540 --> 00:19:56.930
TensorFlow democratized
training models,

00:19:56.930 --> 00:20:01.310
but really training is only a
small part of the whole machine

00:20:01.310 --> 00:20:03.680
learning workflow and cycle.

00:20:03.680 --> 00:20:08.150
In fact, train model
is just one operation

00:20:08.150 --> 00:20:10.777
of this multi-step Pipeline.

00:20:15.040 --> 00:20:15.540
All right.

00:20:15.540 --> 00:20:18.760
So let's check on what
our model is doing.

00:20:23.740 --> 00:20:27.724
There we go.

00:20:27.724 --> 00:20:30.220
Awesome.

00:20:30.220 --> 00:20:36.310
So these schemas basically
are JSON peramiter files

00:20:36.310 --> 00:20:41.530
that tell each operation certain
characteristics about the data,

00:20:41.530 --> 00:20:44.980
like which satellite
it should pull from,

00:20:44.980 --> 00:20:48.830
what date range it should
look for, things like that.

00:20:48.830 --> 00:20:52.630
And that's all we
provide to our Pipeline.

00:20:52.630 --> 00:20:55.420
Right now it's actually
pulling imagery live

00:20:55.420 --> 00:20:57.790
from our Descartes
Labs platform.

00:20:57.790 --> 00:20:59.320
And you can see
it streaming now.

00:21:03.270 --> 00:21:08.850
And this just goes to show
how fast our data access is.

00:21:08.850 --> 00:21:13.110
If you were to try and
do geospatial analysis

00:21:13.110 --> 00:21:15.300
20 years ago, you
would have to work

00:21:15.300 --> 00:21:19.740
with these very bespoke and
oftentimes slow interfaces.

00:21:23.770 --> 00:21:24.270
OK.

00:21:24.270 --> 00:21:26.860
Well, training
image is completed.

00:21:26.860 --> 00:21:31.790
It pushed it to a Google
bucket, so let's check that out.

00:21:31.790 --> 00:21:34.180
Here we go.

00:21:34.180 --> 00:21:38.310
So our V2 images
are in this bucket.

00:21:38.310 --> 00:21:40.290
We can scroll around
and look at that.

00:21:42.850 --> 00:21:47.140
So very briefly, the
machine learning algorithm

00:21:47.140 --> 00:21:50.680
that we're using to train
our oil and well pad detector

00:21:50.680 --> 00:21:54.910
is what we call
core segmentation.

00:21:54.910 --> 00:21:58.480
So the input images
look like this.

00:21:58.480 --> 00:21:59.907
So this is a well pad.

00:22:02.890 --> 00:22:07.910
And the target images
look like this.

00:22:07.910 --> 00:22:13.150
So it's a binary image,
where the well pads are.

00:22:13.150 --> 00:22:15.640
And we notice that
it's much, much smaller

00:22:15.640 --> 00:22:17.500
than the original target image.

00:22:17.500 --> 00:22:22.640
And that's because for simple
shapes like these well pads,

00:22:22.640 --> 00:22:25.010
you don't really need
that high fidelity.

00:22:25.010 --> 00:22:28.070
And because the target
image is really small,

00:22:28.070 --> 00:22:31.000
these models train much faster.

00:22:31.000 --> 00:22:32.830
And you see you'll get
pretty good results

00:22:32.830 --> 00:22:35.330
with a limited amount of data
and a limited amount of epochs

00:22:35.330 --> 00:22:35.830
to train.

00:22:38.430 --> 00:22:40.650
So let's check in
on our Pipeline.

00:22:40.650 --> 00:22:44.240
It will update on the fly.

00:22:44.240 --> 00:22:48.450
It looks like it's training
our model right now.

00:22:48.450 --> 00:22:50.670
What we did was
we pulled imagery

00:22:50.670 --> 00:22:53.970
down from what we've
created in previous steps.

00:22:53.970 --> 00:22:57.510
And it looks like
it's on epoch four.

00:22:57.510 --> 00:22:59.310
And the great thing
about Pipelines

00:22:59.310 --> 00:23:05.320
is that this stuff is
training on the GPU.

00:23:05.320 --> 00:23:08.560
And as long as we have
GDU nodes available,

00:23:08.560 --> 00:23:10.930
Kubeflow will schedule them.

00:23:10.930 --> 00:23:16.030
And what's really great is that
upgrading your GPUS to TPUs

00:23:16.030 --> 00:23:20.203
is just as transparent as adding
TPU nodes to your node pools.

00:23:23.590 --> 00:23:25.907
So this should be done
in a couple minutes.

00:23:25.907 --> 00:23:27.895
We're on epoch seven.

00:23:34.870 --> 00:23:38.310
So while that's training I
can show you very briefly

00:23:38.310 --> 00:23:40.290
what our labels look like.

00:23:46.520 --> 00:23:49.690
So this is our base
satellite imagery.

00:23:49.690 --> 00:23:52.060
And these are our labels.

00:23:52.060 --> 00:23:55.030
We don't have a
whole lot of them.

00:23:55.030 --> 00:24:00.360
But we've found that
these core segmentation

00:24:00.360 --> 00:24:03.450
models train pretty quickly.

00:24:03.450 --> 00:24:05.820
And we can zoom in
on and take a look

00:24:05.820 --> 00:24:08.190
at some of these well pads.

00:24:08.190 --> 00:24:12.540
So this is 60 centimeter
imagery over NAP.

00:24:12.540 --> 00:24:17.110
NAP is a from the National
Agricultural Program.

00:24:17.110 --> 00:24:19.650
So a lot of this
imagery is obstensively

00:24:19.650 --> 00:24:24.150
for agriculture research.

00:24:24.150 --> 00:24:26.990
So I kind of zoomed
in on the well pad.

00:24:26.990 --> 00:24:30.740
You can kind of see
there's a little dark spot

00:24:30.740 --> 00:24:32.300
on the center of these.

00:24:32.300 --> 00:24:36.020
That's where they put the
well when they put the pump

00:24:36.020 --> 00:24:39.310
jack to pump the oil out.

00:24:39.310 --> 00:24:39.810
OK.

00:24:39.810 --> 00:24:44.390
So I think that our training
should be close to done.

00:24:44.390 --> 00:24:44.890
Yeah.

00:24:44.890 --> 00:24:46.990
It looks like it's
saving the model.

00:24:57.880 --> 00:24:59.950
It takes a little
while to upload.

00:24:59.950 --> 00:25:01.298
Good.

00:25:01.298 --> 00:25:04.480
Here we go.

00:25:04.480 --> 00:25:06.890
So let's take a look here.

00:25:09.800 --> 00:25:15.090
And we can see that it saves
our model weights in the cloud.

00:25:15.090 --> 00:25:25.760
And if we go back, we
can see that this metrics

00:25:25.760 --> 00:25:26.870
panel is done.

00:25:26.870 --> 00:25:29.060
So let's take a look at that.

00:25:31.930 --> 00:25:34.988
And we can open up TensorBoard
right from Kubeflow Pipelines.

00:25:37.730 --> 00:25:38.810
So that's great.

00:25:38.810 --> 00:25:40.580
We see that our loss
went down over time.

00:25:48.550 --> 00:25:51.970
And it just launched
our catalog end point.

00:25:51.970 --> 00:25:54.970
That's where we store our data.

00:25:54.970 --> 00:25:59.570
And now it's launching
our task system.

00:25:59.570 --> 00:26:02.311
So this will do the
actual inference.

00:26:05.140 --> 00:26:08.350
So our task takes a
little while to start up.

00:26:08.350 --> 00:26:12.120
And that's just loading
a task controller,

00:26:12.120 --> 00:26:15.240
making sure our deploy
script's in there, distributed

00:26:15.240 --> 00:26:17.610
to the worker nodes.

00:26:17.610 --> 00:26:21.900
And it takes a little bit
of time to spin up the--

00:26:21.900 --> 00:26:25.200
to get the resources.

00:26:25.200 --> 00:26:29.460
So we can see that our
tasks are pending now.

00:26:29.460 --> 00:26:32.302
Once that's done, it should
start the inference job.

00:26:32.302 --> 00:26:33.510
And it should be pretty fast.

00:26:44.422 --> 00:26:45.414
Yeah.

00:26:45.414 --> 00:26:51.890
So our TensorBoard logs
are available here.

00:26:51.890 --> 00:26:55.360
If they're multiple
in one file, you

00:26:55.360 --> 00:26:58.359
can compare the runs
and things like that.

00:27:04.410 --> 00:27:04.910
OK.

00:27:04.910 --> 00:27:07.500
So our task system
says it started.

00:27:07.500 --> 00:27:10.560
And we can look at
our task monitor

00:27:10.560 --> 00:27:13.460
and see that it
is indeed running.

00:27:13.460 --> 00:27:17.300
So it's already scheduling
work on these nodes.

00:27:17.300 --> 00:27:21.560
And look, we already
have 127 active workers.

00:27:21.560 --> 00:27:23.210
And it's already succeeding.

00:27:23.210 --> 00:27:26.210
So as soon as this is done,
we can look at the outputs.

00:27:29.452 --> 00:27:30.910
And it's going
really, really fast.

00:27:30.910 --> 00:27:36.410
[LAUGHS]

00:27:36.410 --> 00:27:36.910
OK.

00:27:36.910 --> 00:27:38.440
So that is completed.

00:27:38.440 --> 00:27:44.970
And Kubeflow should think that
that's completed very soon.

00:27:44.970 --> 00:27:45.470
All right.

00:27:45.470 --> 00:27:46.980
Awesome.

00:27:46.980 --> 00:27:49.860
So now this task,
this component is just

00:27:49.860 --> 00:27:52.440
pulling down imagery
from our catalog product.

00:27:52.440 --> 00:27:55.830
That's where we
deployed our model over,

00:27:55.830 --> 00:27:58.362
and where we store our results.

00:27:58.362 --> 00:27:59.820
And this should be
very quick, too.

00:28:03.490 --> 00:28:06.570
And then once this is done, we
can look at our final results.

00:28:06.570 --> 00:28:08.820
So yeah, this is
incredibly powerful.

00:28:08.820 --> 00:28:14.270
We just took a computer
vision model from no data

00:28:14.270 --> 00:28:18.020
to deployed in less
than 10 minutes.

00:28:18.020 --> 00:28:22.156
And it's completely reproducible
with Kubeflow Pipelines.

00:28:25.830 --> 00:28:26.330
All right.

00:28:26.330 --> 00:28:26.870
Awesome.

00:28:26.870 --> 00:28:28.460
This is done.

00:28:28.460 --> 00:28:29.960
Another neat thing
about Kubeflow

00:28:29.960 --> 00:28:34.180
is that you can embed
arbitrary HTML assets.

00:28:34.180 --> 00:28:35.290
So here we go.

00:28:35.290 --> 00:28:39.920
We can see a tile of what we
predicted, and the results.

00:28:43.880 --> 00:28:49.090
So let's take a
look at our product.

00:28:49.090 --> 00:28:51.982
And this is also in viewer.

00:28:51.982 --> 00:28:54.397
And let me zoom
out a little bit.

00:28:59.010 --> 00:28:59.510
OK.

00:28:59.510 --> 00:29:05.230
So this is our deployed
computer vision model.

00:29:05.230 --> 00:29:10.790
I can overlay the base
imagery, so let do that.

00:29:19.266 --> 00:29:21.254
And then we can compare.

00:29:33.182 --> 00:29:36.120
So it didn't train with
a whole lot of data,

00:29:36.120 --> 00:29:38.100
and it didn't train with
a whole lot of epochs.

00:29:38.100 --> 00:29:42.990
But it looks like it
did learn something.

00:29:42.990 --> 00:29:45.825
And, you know, it's really
easy to start the loop

00:29:45.825 --> 00:29:49.350
back over again, add more data,
retrain, try different models.

00:29:49.350 --> 00:29:54.150
Because we've really
reduced that developer cycle

00:29:54.150 --> 00:29:58.330
exponentially with
Kubeflow Pipelines.

00:29:58.330 --> 00:29:58.830
Awesome.

00:29:58.830 --> 00:30:00.352
So that was the demo.

00:30:00.352 --> 00:30:01.560
We can go back to the slides.

00:30:09.844 --> 00:30:10.344
Maybe.

00:30:18.760 --> 00:30:19.260
OK.

00:30:19.260 --> 00:30:24.420
So I want to wrap up this
talk and contextualize what

00:30:24.420 --> 00:30:30.420
we're doing in the scale of
the science that we're doing.

00:30:30.420 --> 00:30:33.890
And, of course, everybody
likes pretty pictures.

00:30:33.890 --> 00:30:37.700
So these are projects that we've
actually deployed to scale.

00:30:37.700 --> 00:30:43.300
So for example, this is all the
trees in New York City, mapped.

00:30:43.300 --> 00:30:45.940
And if you're interested
in looking at trees,

00:30:45.940 --> 00:30:48.940
this visualization was done by
our awesome creative marketing

00:30:48.940 --> 00:30:50.920
guy, Tim Wallace.

00:30:50.920 --> 00:30:54.070
He has a great "Medium"
article available.

00:30:54.070 --> 00:30:58.247
And in principle, we can map all
the trees in the United States.

00:30:58.247 --> 00:30:59.830
And, of course, this
is really useful.

00:30:59.830 --> 00:31:03.760
Because we can monitor where
deforestation and reforestation

00:31:03.760 --> 00:31:06.158
is happening.

00:31:06.158 --> 00:31:07.700
For something a
little bit different,

00:31:07.700 --> 00:31:09.640
this is a global N02 map.

00:31:09.640 --> 00:31:14.730
So nitrogen dioxide is a
byproduct of combustion.

00:31:14.730 --> 00:31:16.350
And so this map is
really telling us

00:31:16.350 --> 00:31:18.310
where pollution is happening.

00:31:18.310 --> 00:31:21.370
You can see hotspots of
where there's coal burning,

00:31:21.370 --> 00:31:22.300
for example.

00:31:22.300 --> 00:31:24.570
And the hotspot in
South Central Africa

00:31:24.570 --> 00:31:28.700
is they do periodic
vegetation burning.

00:31:28.700 --> 00:31:30.590
So this is something
that you normally

00:31:30.590 --> 00:31:33.590
can't see with the naked
eye, but it becomes very

00:31:33.590 --> 00:31:36.530
visible with certain sensors.

00:31:36.530 --> 00:31:40.980
So polluters beware,
we can see you.

00:31:40.980 --> 00:31:45.998
And this project shows some
of the turnaround and agility

00:31:45.998 --> 00:31:47.290
that we have at Descartes Labs.

00:31:47.290 --> 00:31:49.530
So these are
hundreds of thousands

00:31:49.530 --> 00:31:52.720
of buildings over a
region in North Carolina.

00:31:52.720 --> 00:31:55.830
And this was done overnight
ahead of Hurricane Florence,

00:31:55.830 --> 00:32:00.488
so that we could sort of monitor
and predict how many buildings

00:32:00.488 --> 00:32:01.030
were at risk.

00:32:04.700 --> 00:32:08.660
Just to wrap up, we have
a lot of exciting science

00:32:08.660 --> 00:32:11.270
at Descartes Labs,
mostly related

00:32:11.270 --> 00:32:13.370
to the geospatial domain.

00:32:13.370 --> 00:32:16.070
And Kubeflow Pipelines is
a great resource for us,

00:32:16.070 --> 00:32:18.860
because it allows us
to handle the scale

00:32:18.860 --> 00:32:21.630
and scope of our projects.

00:32:21.630 --> 00:32:24.560
Kubeflow also works really
great with the tools we already

00:32:24.560 --> 00:32:26.540
built on Google Cloud.

00:32:26.540 --> 00:32:28.790
And it's becoming
better and better.

00:32:28.790 --> 00:32:31.140
It's an open source project.

00:32:31.140 --> 00:32:35.480
Everybody is free to support
and contribute to it.

00:32:35.480 --> 00:32:39.930
And there's a lively
community already.

00:32:39.930 --> 00:32:41.820
So here's a little bit
of information on how

00:32:41.820 --> 00:32:44.650
to get started with Kubeflow.

00:32:44.650 --> 00:32:48.790
And here's a little bit
of information about us.

00:32:48.790 --> 00:32:51.010
Thank you for your
participation.

00:32:51.010 --> 00:32:52.510
That's the end of our talk.

00:32:52.510 --> 00:32:54.310
[APPLAUSE]

00:32:54.910 --> 00:32:57.960
[MUSIC PLAYING]

